  {
   "id": "e15",
   "name": "The Present Perfect tense"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "We use the Present Perfect tense for past actions that are linked to now: “I have finished my homework.” (It is done now.) “Ayuk has lost his pen.” (He does not have it now.)\nForm: have / has + past participle. I, you, we, they have; he, she, it has.\nRegular verbs: the past participle is the same as the past: cook → cooked.\nIrregular verbs have their own form: go → gone, eat → eaten, see → seen, write → written, take → taken, buy → bought.\nUseful words: ever, never, already, just, yet. “Have you ever eaten koki?” “I have never seen the sea.” “Has Bih finished yet?”\nNegative: haven't / hasn't + participle. Question: Have / Has + subject + participle?\nWith a finished time (yesterday, last week), use the Simple Past: “I saw him yesterday.”",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "v": "sweep",
   "c": "the yard"
  },
  "problem": "Mother asks Ayuk if the yard is clean. Ayuk swept it this morning. Answer for him with the Present Perfect: “Yes, I ({v}) {c}.”",
  "steps": [
   "The subject is I, so we use have.",
   "{v} is irregular: its past participle is {participle(v)}.",
   "There is no finished time word (like yesterday), and the result is now: the yard is clean."
  ],
  "answer": "Yes, I have {participle(v)} {c}.",
  "checks": [
   "participle(v) == 'swept'"
  ]
 },
 "teach": {
